Machine Learning-Based Multi-Target Drug Discovery for Uric Acid Kidney Stone Treatment

Objective
The primary objective of this study is to develop a machine learning based predictive framework for identifying potential drug compounds for the treatment of uric acid kidney stones. The study focuses on multiple biological targets, namely Xanthine Dehydrogenase (XDH), GLUT9, NLRP3 inflammasome, and AgrA. The specific objectives are:
•	To construct predictive models for identifying active drug compounds.
•	To compare the performance of multiple machine learning algorithms such as Random Forest, XGBoost, and Support Vector Machine.
•	To interpret model predictions using SHAP explainability techniques.
•	To validate predicted compounds through molecular docking studies.
1. Introduction
Uric acid kidney stones are formed due to excessive accumulation of uric acid in the urinary tract, often resulting from metabolic imbalance, reduced urate excretion, or increased purine metabolism. Key proteins such as Xanthine Dehydrogenase play a crucial role in uric acid production, while transporters like GLUT9 regulate urate reabsorption. Additionally, inflammatory pathways involving the NLRP3 inflammasome contribute to disease progression.
Traditional drug discovery methods are time-consuming and expensive. With the advancement of computational techniques, machine learning (ML) approaches have emerged as powerful tools for accelerating drug discovery. These methods enable the prediction of biological activity of compounds based on molecular structure, thereby reducing experimental costs and time.
In this study, a multi-target computational approach is proposed to identify compounds that can simultaneously inhibit multiple targets associated with uric acid kidney stone formation.
2. Dataset
The dataset used in this study was compiled from publicly available chemical and bioactivity databases such as ChEMBL, PubChem, and BindingDB. Due to limitations in direct extraction from certain databases, alternative sources were utilized to obtain relevant bioactivity data. The dataset consists of the following attributes:
•	SMILES (Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry System): Represents the chemical structure of compounds.
•	Biological Activity Values: IC50 values for each target protein.
•	Derived pIC50 Values: Converted from IC50 for normalization.
•	Target Labels: Activity values corresponding to XDH, GLUT9, NLRP3, and AgrA.
Data preprocessing steps included:
•	Removal of duplicate entries
•	Handling missing values
•	Conversion of IC50 to pIC50 values
•	Transformation into classification labels (active/inactive) based on threshold values
Molecular descriptors and fingerprints (Morgan fingerprints of 2048 bits) were generated using RDKit and padel descriptors to serve as input features for the models.
