3. Methodology
The proposed methodology consists of several stages, including data preprocessing, feature extraction, model development, explainability analysis, and validation.
 
Figure 1. Methodology of UAKS Drug Prediction
3.1 Feature Extraction
Molecular fingerprints were generated using the Morgan algorithm, which encodes structural information of compounds into binary vectors. These fingerprints serve as input features for machine learning models.
3.2 Model Development
Three machine learning models were implemented:
•	Random Forest Classifier: An ensemble learning method that constructs multiple decision trees.
•	XGBoost Classifier: A gradient boosting algorithm known for high predictive performance.
•	Support Vector Machine (SVM): A classification algorithm that separates data using optimal hyperplanes.
3.3 Model Training and Evaluation
The dataset was split into training and testing sets using an 80:20 ratio and various cross validation. Model performance was evaluated using:
•	Accuracy
•	Cross-validation scores

3.4 Model Explainability
SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) was used to interpret model predictions. SHAP values provide insights into the contribution of each molecular feature toward the prediction outcome, enabling identification of key structural components responsible for activity.
3.5 Molecular Docking
Top predicted compounds were subjected to molecular docking using AutoDock Vina. Docking simulations were performed against all selected protein targets to evaluate binding affinity and interaction stability.
4. Experiments and Data Interpretation
The models were trained and tested using the prepared dataset, and their performance was compared.
•	The Random Forest model demonstrated stable performance with good generalization ability.
•	XGBoost achieved the highest accuracy due to its boosting mechanism.
•	SVM showed competitive performance but required careful feature scaling.
