SHAP analysis revealed that specific molecular fingerprint features significantly influenced prediction outcomes. These features correspond to important chemical substructures responsible for biological activity.
Docking results further validated the predicted compounds, showing favourable binding energies with the target proteins. Compounds such as Apigenin and related phytochemicals exhibited strong interactions, supporting their potential as therapeutic candidates.
5. Conclusion
This study presents a comprehensive machine learning-based framework for the prediction of drug compounds targeting uric acid kidney stone formation. The integration of multiple machine learning models, and explainability methods provides a robust approach for drug discovery.
The results demonstrate that:
•	Machine learning models can effectively predict biologically active compounds.
•	Multi-target prediction enhances the relevance of identified drug candidates.
•	SHAP analysis improves interpretability and reliability of predictions.
•	Molecular docking validates the computational findings.
The proposed approach offers a cost-effective and efficient strategy for identifying potential therapeutic compounds, which can be further validated through experimental studies.
